window.__LDNDRC_PAGES = Object.assign(window.__LDNDRC_PAGES || {}, {"why-k3s":"<p>Kubernetes is the right shape for this problem: it already knows how to schedule a pod onto whichever machine has room, restart it when it dies, and hold a volume next to it. The question was never <em>whether</em> Kubernetes. It was how much of it you can afford on a laptop.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-k3s-removes\">What K3s removes</h2><p>K3s is a single binary under 100 MB that packages the API server, scheduler, controller manager, kubelet, and containerd. It replaces etcd with a small embedded datastore by default and ships sensible defaults for the things a laptop does not have: no cloud provider, no load balancer controller, no CSI driver zoo.</p>\n<p>The practical result: <code>curl -sfL https://get.k3s.io | sh -</code> is the entire control plane install. On the master laptop. No container to bootstrap the container runtime.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-keeps\">What it keeps</h2><p>Everything that matters for this design:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Namespaces</strong> — the tenancy boundary.</li>\n<li><strong>Deployments and Services</strong> — one per student session.</li>\n<li><strong>PersistentVolumeClaims</strong> — the student&#39;s home directory.</li>\n<li><strong>RBAC</strong> — the control panel is namespace-scoped on purpose.</li>\n<li><strong>Ingress</strong> — Traefik is bundled, so the front door exists out of the box.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"why-not-kubeadm-or-a-managed-cluster\">Why not kubeadm or a managed cluster?</h2><p><code>kubeadm</code> is a two-hour install with certificate rotation you have to think about. A managed cluster is a cloud bill and an internet dependency, both ruled out by <a href=\"#/lan-first\" class=\"topic-link\">The LAN-first constraint</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-one-honest-weakness\">The one honest weakness</h2><p>K3s with an embedded datastore is a single control-plane node. If the master dies, the cluster is gone until it comes back. There is no failover, and the roadmap does not pretend otherwise. For a workshop, the recovery plan is &quot;rebuild the master and re-add the Hosts&quot;, which takes about ten minutes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">k3s --version\nkubectl get nodes -o wide</code></pre></div><p>One server, zero control-plane taints to worry about. Next: <a href=\"#/k3d-demo\" class=\"topic-link\">The one-laptop demo (k3d)</a> runs the same thing without a second machine.</p>","k3d-demo":"<p>You should be able to see the whole platform before you own a cluster. <code>k3d</code> runs K3s inside Docker, so a single laptop can host the control plane, the demo image, the ingress, and a working session in about ten minutes.</p>\n<p>The demo deliberately does <strong>not</strong> use the real 10 GB workspace image. It uses <code>ldndrc/demo-standin</code>, a tiny HTTP server that answers on the same ports. That is enough to prove the provisioning path, the gateway, and the UI without waiting on a GPU.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-short-version\">The short version</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\"><span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1. cluster</span>\nk3d cluster create ldndrc -p <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;80:80@loadbalancer&quot;</span> -p <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;443:443@loadbalancer&quot;</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2. images</span>\ndocker build -t ldndrc/control-panel:dev ./control-panel\ndocker build -t ldndrc/demo-standin:dev ./images/demo-standin\nk3d image import ldndrc/control-panel:dev ldndrc/demo-standin:dev -c ldndrc\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 3. control plane</span>\nkubectl create namespace ldndrc\nkubectl -n ldndrc create secret generic ldndrc-control-panel \\\n  --from-literal=JWT_SECRET=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;<span class=\"hljs-subst\">$(openssl rand -hex 32)</span>&quot;</span>\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-rbac.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-deployment.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-service.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-ingress.yaml</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"the-part-people-skip-then-regret\">The part people skip, then regret</h2><p><code>k3d image import</code> is not a registry. The image is copied into the cluster&#39;s containerd store at that moment. If you rebuild the tag and forget to re-import, the Deployment keeps running the old image and the failure looks like &quot;my code change did nothing&quot;.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-the-demo-is-configured-differently\">Why the demo is configured differently</h2><p>Two environment overrides matter:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Real cluster</th>\n<th>Demo</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>SESSION_IMAGE</code></td>\n<td><code>ldndrc/ros2-gz:jazzy-harmonic-&lt;sha&gt;</code></td>\n<td><code>ldndrc/demo-standin:dev</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SESSION_GPU_LIMIT</code></td>\n<td><code>1</code></td>\n<td><code>0</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><code>SESSION_GPU_LIMIT=0</code> means the session schedules on any node with no GPU request, so a laptop without an NVIDIA card still reaches <code>ready</code>. The GPU path is <a href=\"#/gpu-timeslicing\" class=\"topic-link\">Time-slicing one GPU</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"teardown-is-one-command\">Teardown is one command</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">k3d cluster delete ldndrc</code></pre></div><p>Everything goes: workloads, volumes, secrets. That is the beauty of the demo — it is disposable, so you can break it while learning. The real cluster in <a href=\"#/control-panel-deploy\" class=\"topic-link\">Deploying the control panel</a> is not.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">k3d cluster list\nkubectl -n ldndrc get pods</code></pre></div><p>Then follow <a href=\"#/route/goal-demo\" class=\"topic-link\">Demo it on one laptop</a> if you want the guided route through this.</p>","networking":"<p>Two networking components do all the work, and both are easy to misread.</p>\n<h2 id=\"flannel-the-pod-network\">Flannel — the pod network</h2><p>Flannel is the CNI. It gives every pod an IP address and makes pods routable to each other inside the cluster. It is also the component that makes <a href=\"#/dds-isolation\" class=\"topic-link\">Why DDS never leaves the pod</a> true: Flannel&#39;s overlay carries IP traffic, but DDS discovery never generates any because the processes are isolated in their pod namespace.</p>\n<p>Flannel is a deliberate choice for what it <em>doesn&#39;t</em> do. No network policy engine, no service mesh, no eBPF complexity. The isolation model does not depend on filtering packets; it depends on packets never being generated across the boundary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"traefik-the-front-door\">Traefik — the front door</h2><p>Traefik is bundled with K3s and listens on the cluster&#39;s ports 80 and 443. Its job is simple: read the <code>Host</code> header and route to a Service.</p>\n<p>The four hostnames are the whole public surface:</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"text\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">text</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-text\">control.ros-platform.local   → control panel :8082\neditor.ros-platform.local    → gateway → session pod :7682\ndesktop.ros-platform.local   → gateway → session pod :8080\ngazebo.ros-platform.local    → gateway → session pod :9002</code></pre></div><p>Only <code>control.</code> points at a fixed workload. The other three point at the control panel too — the panel is the gateway, and it decides which pod to proxy to based on the logged-in cookie. That indirection is <a href=\"#/gateway-proxy\" class=\"topic-link\">The cookie gateway</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"name-resolution\">Name resolution</h2><p>There is no DNS server in the design. Guests resolve the four names one of two ways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>/etc/hosts</code> entries pointing at the master&#39;s LAN IP</li>\n<li>mDNS advertisement from the master (see <a href=\"#/join-binary\" class=\"topic-link\">The join binary</a>&#39;s discovery path)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On a real LAN you also need the master reachable from every Guest. That means a static IP and a router that does not isolate clients — many venue Wi-Fis do, and that is a real failure mode.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n kube-system get svc traefik\nkubectl get ingress -A</code></pre></div><p>An ingress with no address is not routing yet. Read its events before touching the panel.</p>","control-panel-deploy":"<p>The control panel is a single Go binary. It is the only stateful brain in the system: it authenticates users, provisions sessions, proxies tool traffic, and approves laptops. Deploying it is four manifests and one secret.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-gets-applied\">What gets applied</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl create namespace ldndrc\nkubectl -n ldndrc create secret generic ldndrc-control-panel \\\n  --from-literal=JWT_SECRET=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;<span class=\"hljs-subst\">$(openssl rand -hex 32)</span>&quot;</span>\n\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-rbac.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-deployment.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-service.yaml\nkubectl apply -f manifests/control-panel-ingress.yaml</code></pre></div><h2 id=\"the-rbac-decision-that-matters\">The RBAC decision that matters</h2><p>The panel&#39;s ServiceAccount is <strong>namespace-scoped</strong>. It can create and delete Deployments, Services, and PVCs in <code>ldndrc</code>. It cannot list nodes cluster-wide, and it cannot touch other namespaces.</p>\n<p>That is enforced by a set of <code>auth can-i</code> checks that are part of the deployment checklist:</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc auth can-i create deployments \\\n  --as=system:serviceaccount:ldndrc:ldndrc-control-panel   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># expect: yes</span>\nkubectl -n ldndrc auth can-i delete persistentvolumeclaims \\\n  --as=system:serviceaccount:ldndrc:ldndrc-control-panel   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># expect: yes</span>\nkubectl -n ldndrc auth can-i list nodes \\\n  --as=system:serviceaccount:ldndrc:ldndrc-control-panel   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># expect: no</span></code></pre></div><p>The third one is the one people get wrong. The node onboarding feature needs a separate, narrowly-scoped nodes permission — not a cluster-admin shortcut.</p>\n<h2 id=\"configuration-is-environment-variables\">Configuration is environment variables</h2><p>Everything the panel needs to know is injected, not baked in:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Why it exists</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>JWT_SECRET</code></td>\n<td>Signs tokens and the gateway cookie; the process exits if unset</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SESSION_IMAGE</code></td>\n<td>Which workspace image to run</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SESSION_HOME_STORAGE</code></td>\n<td>PVC size per student, default <code>5Gi</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>SESSION_GPU_LIMIT</code></td>\n<td><code>1</code> for real Hosts, <code>0</code> for the demo</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>K3S_JOIN_URL</code></td>\n<td>Where approved laptops should send their agent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>COOKIE_DOMAIN</code></td>\n<td><code>.ros-platform.local</code>, so one login covers four hosts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"rolling-it-out\">Rolling it out</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc rollout status deployment/ldndrc-control-panel\nkubectl -n ldndrc port-forward svc/ldndrc-control-panel 8082:8082 &amp;\ncurl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/healthz</code></pre></div><p>On a one-service-port cluster the rollout can briefly have zero available replicas. That is why the panel&#39;s persistence and probe settings are not cosmetic — see <a href=\"#/durable-state\" class=\"topic-link\">Surviving a redeploy</a> for what happens when they are wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc rollout status deployment/ldndrc-control-panel\nkubectl -n ldndrc get deploy,svc,ingress</code></pre></div>","headlamp":"<p>Headlamp is a Kubernetes dashboard deployed as a normal manifest. It exists so the operator can answer &quot;what is actually running?&quot; without composing <code>kubectl</code> output by hand.</p>\n<div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl apply -f manifests/headlamp.yaml\nkubectl -n ldndrc port-forward svc/ldndrc-headlamp 8080:80 &amp;\nkubectl -n ldndrc create token ldndrc-headlamp --duration=24h</code></pre></div><p>Paste the token into <code>http://localhost:8080</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-is-good-for\">What it is good for</h2><ul>\n<li>Seeing session pods appear and disappear while a class is provisioning.</li>\n<li>Reading events on a <code>Pending</code> pod without switching terminals.</li>\n<li>Showing the cluster to someone who does not live in a shell.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"what-it-is-not\">What it is not</h2><p>It is not the student-facing UI, and it is not exposed to Guests. It is an operator tool, reachable by port-forward only. The &quot;glass wall&quot; metaphor in the journey report is literal: you can see in, students cannot.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl -n ldndrc get svc ldndrc-headlamp</code></pre></div>","manifests-layout":"<p>The <code>manifests/</code> directory is the whole declared surface of the platform. If it is not in here, it does not exist in a fresh cluster.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>File</th>\n<th>Object</th>\n<th>Notes</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>control-panel-rbac.yaml</code></td>\n<td>ServiceAccount, Role, RoleBinding</td>\n<td>namespace-scoped on purpose</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>control-panel-deployment.yaml</code></td>\n<td>Deployment</td>\n<td>image, env, probes, volumes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>control-panel-service.yaml</code></td>\n<td>Service</td>\n<td>port <code>8082</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>control-panel-ingress.yaml</code></td>\n<td>Ingress</td>\n<td>the four hostnames</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>headlamp.yaml</code></td>\n<td>Deployment + Service</td>\n<td>operator dashboard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>control-panel-hostnetwork-patch.yaml</code></td>\n<td>patch</td>\n<td>optional, for mDNS on real masters</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>secret.example.yaml</code></td>\n<td>docs</td>\n<td>shape of the JWT secret</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"the-rule-for-adding-a-file\">The rule for adding a file</h2><p>One concern per file, and the filename says which concern. A manifest that both patches and deploys is a manifest nobody can reason about during an incident.</p>\n<h2 id=\"check-it-yourself\">Check it yourself</h2><div class=\"code term\" data-lang=\"bash\"><span class=\"lang-tag\">bash</span><button class=\"copy\" data-copy aria-label=\"Copy code\"><svg aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#i-copy\"/></svg></button><pre><code class=\"hljs language-bash\">kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f manifests/</code></pre></div>"});
